1
00:00:02,127 --> 00:00:07,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Perhaps we can discuss how to spend time in seclusion wisely.

2
00:00:09,638 --> 00:00:15,086
[SPEAKER_01]: And more particularly, how do you find out if you're acting out of distraction?

3
00:00:16,743 --> 00:00:21,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Because distraction and entertainment are not necessarily the same.

4
00:00:21,867 --> 00:00:24,509
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems to me distraction is a much wider category.

5
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:30,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Movies, music, news can be on the side of entertainment.

6
00:00:31,234 --> 00:00:35,678
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not that if you refrain from those things that you're automatically not distracting yourself.

7
00:00:36,798 --> 00:00:55,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do people who come here, for example, where there's little activity going on, outside of some work which is really necessary, or people who live by themselves and have a fair amount of time secluded from other people, how to take advantage of those circumstances?

8
00:00:58,323 --> 00:00:58,483
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

9
00:00:58,583 --> 00:01:03,749
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the first step would obviously be to abandon entertainment.

10
00:01:04,310 --> 00:01:13,661
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it is true that distraction is a wider category than entertainment, but you can be very sure that if you're engaging with entertainment, you are distracting yourself.

11
00:01:13,681 --> 00:01:14,822
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no question about that.

12
00:01:15,943 --> 00:01:19,605
[SPEAKER_03]: So, to that extent, you have accomplished yourself in non-distraction.

13
00:01:19,705 --> 00:01:31,253
[SPEAKER_03]: If you were able to abandon those things, and not depend on them anymore, that is a better position when it comes to your liability to distraction.

14
00:01:31,353 --> 00:01:45,522
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, the problem is that then, that category is so much wider, that you would have to realize that even things that you think are wholesome, actually fall within distraction.

15
00:01:47,023 --> 00:01:48,463
[SPEAKER_03]: Even though they're not against the precepts.

16
00:01:48,723 --> 00:02:01,588
[SPEAKER_03]: Because distraction is more, much more than just obvious activities that you, you know, at the level of external things that you can either, that you can just stop and and just give it up.

17
00:02:02,649 --> 00:02:09,051
[SPEAKER_03]: Your own mind, the momentum of your own mind, is by default going towards distraction.

18
00:02:10,670 --> 00:02:20,412
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's why when you would... let's say you've been properly established in the precepts and sense restraint, and then you go into seclusion,

19
00:02:22,039 --> 00:02:33,444
[SPEAKER_03]: What's going to feel easier is to spend all that time engaged in some kind of mental activity.

20
00:02:33,644 --> 00:02:48,490
[SPEAKER_03]: So, study, yeah, you would or you might be rehearsing and bringing up ideas that you read about or heard about, which is not a problem in itself, but we'll get back to it.

21
00:02:49,370 --> 00:02:55,675
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, you know, for some people, they might spend time doing a meditation technique, which is also a distraction.

22
00:02:56,575 --> 00:03:07,003
[SPEAKER_03]: They might then... Or if you're not making any effort at all, you just spend time remembering things about the past and planning for the future.

23
00:03:08,784 --> 00:03:14,087
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, you know, worrying about what other people think about you and what happened today and what's going to happen tomorrow.

24
00:03:15,128 --> 00:03:22,651
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you will notice that it's not something that you that you do because you want it to happen necessarily.

25
00:03:22,672 --> 00:03:28,414
[SPEAKER_03]: You would realize that your mind has its own momentum that prefers distraction, prefers activity.

26
00:03:29,655 --> 00:03:38,999
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's the crucial thing to realize because then you realize that it won't be a matter of a simple decision to just stop the distraction.

27
00:03:40,596 --> 00:03:43,759
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that could become your activity.

28
00:03:43,919 --> 00:03:49,004
[SPEAKER_03]: That could become your thing through which you overlook where your mind actually is.

29
00:03:49,104 --> 00:03:53,167
[SPEAKER_03]: That kind of constant reasserting.

30
00:03:53,207 --> 00:03:53,368
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

31
00:03:53,728 --> 00:03:53,988
[SPEAKER_03]: Stop.

32
00:03:54,228 --> 00:03:54,489
[SPEAKER_03]: Stop.

33
00:03:54,629 --> 00:03:54,909
[SPEAKER_03]: Stop.

34
00:03:55,349 --> 00:03:58,973
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, that's why, even when people think that they're not thinking,

35
00:04:01,342 --> 00:04:17,292
[SPEAKER_03]: That doesn't at all mean that they're inactive, because you can be suppressing your thoughts, or you will be suppressing your thoughts as an activity, and that's what is distracting you from being aware of what you actually need to be aware of, which is your motivation.

36
00:04:17,532 --> 00:04:20,835
[SPEAKER_03]: Why am I trying to suppress my thoughts, for example?

37
00:04:21,935 --> 00:04:29,220
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the first step then, rather than trying to blanket stop all activities,

38
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:40,827
[SPEAKER_03]: is to start becoming aware of the motivation behind whatever activities remain once the obviously unwholesome activities have been removed.

39
00:04:42,147 --> 00:04:56,956
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, that you realize that will be a much more subtle game, if you want to put it that way, of almost like a cat-and-mouse game, where it won't be necessarily right in your face.

40
00:04:57,772 --> 00:05:04,958
[SPEAKER_03]: Where the distraction is, where the mind is still kind of leaning in these unwholesome directions, it won't be obvious.

41
00:05:06,118 --> 00:05:19,489
[SPEAKER_03]: But, by emphasizing the motivation, by asking yourself, if the mind is thinking about X, instead of automatically seeing a problem with that, you ask yourself, why is it thinking about X?

42
00:05:20,389 --> 00:05:21,050
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it passion?

43
00:05:21,791 --> 00:05:23,092
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it obsessed with something?

44
00:05:23,772 --> 00:05:24,673
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it kind of...

45
00:05:26,555 --> 00:05:30,277
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it clearly invested in things that are not in in my control?

46
00:05:31,357 --> 00:05:37,860
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, if you see that, you see, okay, I will stop it because of that, not because the thought in itself is a distraction.

47
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:47,744
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you know, through that, basically, you you won't see distraction as a thing itself, especially not in the beginning.

48
00:05:48,324 --> 00:05:54,207
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to approach it indirectly through, like, these specific ways that it will manifest.

49
00:05:54,647 --> 00:06:04,353
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you could say sensual desire, aversion, laziness, restlessness, doubt, the five hindrances, those will be your destruction.

50
00:06:04,734 --> 00:06:13,139
[SPEAKER_03]: Those will be the pressures that will be taking you away from recognizing the presence of those very pressures.

51
00:06:15,741 --> 00:06:19,543
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, that's where you would start undoing it.

52
00:06:23,038 --> 00:06:28,721
[SPEAKER_03]: As an indirect thing, rather than just as a choice to not distract yourself.

53
00:06:29,182 --> 00:06:44,650
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you realize, once you remove all these things, by not acting out of them, not by trying to suppress them, then you automatically be non-distracted, without you trying to fabricate the non-distraction as a thing that you do, because that will never work.

54
00:06:46,147 --> 00:06:54,333
[SPEAKER_03]: As long as you're putting your point of view and your point of... and your volition first, that's the thing that will be distracting you.

55
00:06:54,353 --> 00:07:01,979
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, it will be constricting the picture and covering up the full scope of what's happening internally.

56
00:07:02,939 --> 00:07:07,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, just as boredom, for example, is not something that we can volitionally produce.

57
00:07:07,161 --> 00:07:10,682
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot say I'm going to do this and that, and then I'm going to be bored.

58
00:07:11,022 --> 00:07:15,603
[SPEAKER_01]: It will arise naturally on account of not doing a whole bunch of other things.

59
00:07:16,083 --> 00:07:16,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

60
00:07:16,604 --> 00:07:20,165
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, if that starts to arise, can we conclude that that's necessarily wholesome?

61
00:07:20,765 --> 00:07:23,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Can we say that feeling bored is okay?

62
00:07:23,586 --> 00:07:27,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is there still some sort of more subtle element of resistance involved?

63
00:07:27,262 --> 00:07:46,716
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, well, it can be a sign of... definitely, if you were somebody who was recently, quite obviously, giving in to unwholesome things, then boredom will be a symptom that you are... that you have ceased feeding those unwholesome things.

64
00:07:46,756 --> 00:07:49,418
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, it's only revealing you what's already there.

65
00:07:50,059 --> 00:07:56,103
[SPEAKER_03]: It's revealing you the pressure and the momentum of mind that you would have otherwise just continued with.

66
00:07:57,284 --> 00:08:20,606
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, in that instance, it would be quite important to recognize, it's not that the restraint, you know, when you said boredom, like, that's exactly what would come up when, for example, somebody who's been not restraining themselves from entertainment, even though they might have been doing relatively well in other regards,

67
00:08:22,233 --> 00:08:36,346
[SPEAKER_03]: The boredom would still come up, because there is some minimal degree of activity, of kind of staying in touch with the world, that the mind, that an untrained mind, would need to feel at ease.

68
00:08:36,406 --> 00:08:44,994
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, cutting that off, it's not that it would create, that it's not that it's creating the pain, it's not that it's creating

69
00:08:47,748 --> 00:08:58,933
[SPEAKER_03]: As something that wasn't there before, it's just revealing the, you could say, infection or disease of the pressure that was already there the whole time.

70
00:08:59,373 --> 00:09:08,677
[SPEAKER_01]: And all these previous activities were perhaps motivated by not wanting to feel that original, quote-unquote, this quiet which was there the whole time.

71
00:09:09,677 --> 00:09:18,059
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, that's quite important, because then it would become clear to you that you have no way, no escape from it.

72
00:09:18,739 --> 00:09:27,902
[SPEAKER_03]: You're either acting out of it, making it worse in the meantime, or you stop acting out of it and you start doing something about it, but you can't.

73
00:09:28,722 --> 00:09:32,843
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it almost feels like, when you distract yourself,

74
00:09:34,539 --> 00:09:39,243
[SPEAKER_03]: Like you kind of... like it's not there anymore, the pressure of the border, feels like.

75
00:09:40,284 --> 00:09:48,610
[SPEAKER_03]: But the fact is, you realize immediately, when you stop distracting yourself, oh, it didn't go away, because otherwise, how could it get worse?

76
00:09:49,070 --> 00:09:50,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the meaning of the distraction.

77
00:09:50,772 --> 00:09:52,153
[SPEAKER_01]: You were distracted from it.

78
00:09:52,173 --> 00:09:52,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

79
00:09:52,493 --> 00:09:55,896
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through distraction, you can never resolve the original problem.

80
00:09:55,916 --> 00:09:56,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

81
00:09:56,036 --> 00:09:56,796
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the whole point now.

82
00:09:56,816 --> 00:09:56,976
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

83
00:09:57,037 --> 00:10:03,942
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, the more you train that, the more you basically catch yourself being already distracted, because that's how it's going to work.

84
00:10:04,382 --> 00:10:07,164
[SPEAKER_03]: You won't be able to kind of catch the first point.

85
00:10:07,424 --> 00:10:11,207
[SPEAKER_03]: Otherwise, you wouldn't have ended there in the first place.

86
00:10:12,008 --> 00:10:21,975
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you start to realize, actually, this promise of pleasure that comes from distraction is actually just a trap.

87
00:10:22,235 --> 00:10:25,398
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not really that you're getting what you think you're getting.

88
00:10:26,358 --> 00:10:29,560
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, you think that you are resolving this internal pressure.

89
00:10:30,321 --> 00:10:31,041
[SPEAKER_03]: Actually, you're not.

90
00:10:31,601 --> 00:10:38,726
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is why, again, like you take anybody who, I don't know, was was pressured by boredom and then they started watching a movie.

91
00:10:39,366 --> 00:10:41,247
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the power goes out in the meantime.

92
00:10:42,368 --> 00:10:44,089
[SPEAKER_03]: You will see that the pressure will be worse.

93
00:10:44,489 --> 00:10:44,709
[SPEAKER_03]: Mm-hmm.

94
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:50,054
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you know, if the power had gone out before... Yeah, or it turns out to be a really bad movie.

95
00:10:50,454 --> 00:10:50,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

96
00:10:50,715 --> 00:10:59,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then... Because then, indirectly, you'll be automatically reminded that you were distracting yourself, and that intention is painful.

97
00:10:59,442 --> 00:11:02,925
[SPEAKER_03]: It will bring you back to, like, the pressure that you were feeling the whole time.

98
00:11:02,985 --> 00:11:03,205
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

99
00:11:04,126 --> 00:11:11,409
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, if you become clear about that, you realize, well, I can't escape from this.

100
00:11:11,989 --> 00:11:20,653
[SPEAKER_03]: I can either pretend that it's not there, and again keep making it worse, or I can endure this pain, which is there anyway,

101
00:11:21,813 --> 00:11:29,336
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, you know, going the way of restraint gives you actually hope of overcoming that pain altogether.

102
00:11:29,796 --> 00:11:36,098
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas the other way, like, you will have the pain anyway, but there's no chance that you will ever overcome it by giving into distraction.

103
00:11:37,539 --> 00:11:48,603
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, that would be the training, as you said, or as we began, for somebody who has been established in the other things, because otherwise,

104
00:11:49,625 --> 00:11:51,108
[SPEAKER_03]: All of this is just going to be abstract.

105
00:11:51,549 --> 00:11:58,165
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's not a coincidence that when somebody hasn't developed themselves in the precepts enough, and in sense restraint enough,

106
00:12:00,218 --> 00:12:02,818
[SPEAKER_03]: They only feel like they have two options when they're in seclusion.

107
00:12:03,359 --> 00:12:13,700
[SPEAKER_03]: Either distract themselves with, most commonly, with a meditation technique, or their mind is erasing left and right, you know, completely out of control, and there's nothing in between.

108
00:12:14,600 --> 00:12:29,463
[SPEAKER_03]: Precisely because they haven't fulfilled these more basic prerequisites that would allow them to see the choice of distraction on the right level, and just refrain from that, instead of trying to overly determine

109
00:12:30,663 --> 00:12:36,625
[SPEAKER_03]: Concentration, for example, to like just keep the mind from going there.

110
00:12:36,665 --> 00:12:47,327
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like you don't have to, I don't know, you don't have to play very loud music in order for your shouts not to be heard.

111
00:12:48,228 --> 00:12:49,268
[SPEAKER_03]: You just have to stop shouting.

112
00:12:53,305 --> 00:12:57,488
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't have to play loud music so that other people don't hear you shouting.

113
00:12:58,188 --> 00:12:59,289
[SPEAKER_03]: You just have to stop shouting.

114
00:13:00,230 --> 00:13:12,359
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in the same sense, you don't need to resolve and do focusing or do some kind of mental activity that you think is the practice.

115
00:13:12,859 --> 00:13:20,082
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to prevent the unwholesome things, you just have to be aware of where the unwholesome things are, on the right level, and just not engage with it.

116
00:13:20,102 --> 00:13:20,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, yeah.

117
00:13:20,502 --> 00:13:25,524
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, it just seemed like the way you said it, that shouting is like a choice.

118
00:13:25,544 --> 00:13:26,124
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

119
00:13:26,164 --> 00:13:27,865
[SPEAKER_02]: It's supposed to be like a... but is it?

120
00:13:28,145 --> 00:13:31,306
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I thought shouting is... you tackle the shouting indirectly.

121
00:13:31,586 --> 00:13:32,486
[SPEAKER_02]: Or maybe I actually did.

122
00:13:32,546 --> 00:13:32,806
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no.

123
00:13:32,906 --> 00:13:37,908
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... it's... well, maybe, yeah, it's worth clarifying.

124
00:13:37,968 --> 00:13:39,449
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that you can stop the...

125
00:13:40,462 --> 00:13:43,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Momentum of the mind's pressure as a choice.

126
00:13:43,703 --> 00:13:45,023
[SPEAKER_00]: The same way you can stop the shouting.

127
00:13:45,744 --> 00:13:47,164
[SPEAKER_00]: But either way, it's the same.

128
00:13:47,184 --> 00:13:49,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, you might have the pressure to keep shouting.

129
00:13:49,325 --> 00:13:49,785
[SPEAKER_02]: I understood.

130
00:13:49,825 --> 00:13:51,986
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the meditation technique is the shouting.

131
00:13:52,086 --> 00:13:52,306
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

132
00:13:52,466 --> 00:14:08,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Which you can clearly... Or that, or might be, you know, for some people, it might just be like overly clarifying and revolving around ideas that... You know, if you kind of stop and ask yourself, oh, is this actually purifying my mind from unwholesome states?

133
00:14:09,051 --> 00:14:13,813
[SPEAKER_00]: The way that this is to say reflection should, like right reflection should, you realize it's not.

134
00:14:13,893 --> 00:14:27,457
[SPEAKER_03]: It's very often making it worse, winding me up with more doubt and more pressure, which means this can't be what the Buddha meant with, you know, reflecting on the dhamma that you've heard as a thing that would purify you, actually, from unwholesome states.

135
00:14:28,437 --> 00:14:31,378
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's something I mentioned before.

136
00:14:31,438 --> 00:14:37,040
[SPEAKER_03]: It's, it's, eh... you, you have to learn the line also, because that's one thing that

137
00:14:39,446 --> 00:14:40,787
[SPEAKER_03]: Would be part of the solution.

138
00:14:40,967 --> 00:14:53,057
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, reflecting, contemplating, reminding yourself of the right themes, would be a way to lean your mind away from this default tendency to distract itself.

139
00:14:53,878 --> 00:14:55,659
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, reflect on the danger of this.

140
00:14:56,180 --> 00:14:58,302
[SPEAKER_03]: Reflect on all what we just said.

141
00:14:58,342 --> 00:14:59,703
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, the pain is there anyway.

142
00:15:00,183 --> 00:15:01,564
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not actually being resolved.

143
00:15:02,485 --> 00:15:10,647
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, keep reminding yourself of that every time you feel the pressure to distract yourself, and that is going to contribute to your ability to not give in to it.

144
00:15:10,707 --> 00:15:18,589
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, the difficulty is, like, you will have to be aware, again, as I've said before, your intention.

145
00:15:19,610 --> 00:15:30,853
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if you are clear, okay, I am contemplating the dhamma, because I see my mind leaning in this direction, and I'm trying to rein it back in,

146
00:15:32,213 --> 00:15:34,415
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a wholesome reason to contemplate.

147
00:15:34,915 --> 00:15:45,324
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, at other times, you realize, no, no, what the mind wants is the clarification, is the thinking, is the revolving around these ideas.

148
00:15:45,484 --> 00:15:47,786
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, that becomes your distraction.

149
00:15:48,207 --> 00:15:49,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Depending on the motivation behind it.

150
00:15:49,668 --> 00:15:52,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it could be acting out of the hindrance of doubt, for example.

151
00:15:52,470 --> 00:16:07,065
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, I mean, that applies to everything, but this is a case that quite illustrates it quite well, how wholesome and unwholesome is not about the things, it's about why are you doing it at that time.

152
00:16:07,765 --> 00:16:13,471
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, contemplation is a perfect example, because even the suttas talk about it, and it is

153
00:16:13,731 --> 00:16:18,976
[SPEAKER_03]: It is the way that you would even develop jhāna in many of the suttas it's described.

154
00:16:19,796 --> 00:16:24,460
[SPEAKER_03]: And yet, if you do it wrongly, i.e.

155
00:16:24,520 --> 00:16:30,465
[SPEAKER_03]: if you do it with the wrong motivation, it will be precisely what would be keeping you, keeping your mind defiled.

156
00:16:30,485 --> 00:16:32,207
[SPEAKER_03]: It would be the gateway for your acting out.

157
00:16:32,327 --> 00:16:32,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

158
00:16:33,308 --> 00:16:39,013
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, should you clear up whether there's a difference between motivation and intention?

159
00:16:39,494 --> 00:16:43,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Can we say that intention is most generally greed, hate, delusion?

160
00:16:44,538 --> 00:16:45,539
[SPEAKER_01]: And delusion, right?

161
00:16:45,819 --> 00:16:50,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, let's say, five hindrances, because I could be motivated to become enlightened, say.

162
00:16:53,062 --> 00:16:55,844
[SPEAKER_01]: And then start using that motivation to justify all sorts of things.

163
00:16:56,244 --> 00:17:03,208
[SPEAKER_01]: But that should be refined, because that's never going to happen, as long as they're still acting out of an awesome state.

164
00:17:03,448 --> 00:17:06,570
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but the motivation would be...

165
00:17:06,731 --> 00:17:08,752
[SPEAKER_03]: I guess it's just semantics, but...

166
00:17:09,152 --> 00:17:12,735
[SPEAKER_03]: When I say motivation, I don't mean like a rational explanation that you give.

167
00:17:12,915 --> 00:17:13,236
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

168
00:17:13,476 --> 00:17:23,445
